The Wallis product is a famous formula from the 17th century discovered by mathematician John Wallis. 
It is one of the earliest infinite product formulas in mathematics and is closely related to the value of pi.
You need to write a program in simplecpp to compute an approximation of pi using the Wallis product for a fixed number of terms. 
You need to take an integer N as input and you need to calculate the Wallis Product up to N terms. 
Then you need to print the approximation of the value of Pi. Each multiplication in the given formula is counted as 1 term. 
Note: N is even.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int N;
    cin >> N;

    double product = 1.0;

    for (int k = 1; k <= N / 2; k++) {
        product *= (2.0 * k) / (2.0 * k - 1);
        product *= (2.0 * k) / (2.0 * k + 1);
    }

    double pi = 2 * product;
    cout << pi;

    return 0;
}
